   1:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c **** /*
   2:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * jfdctflt.c
   3:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  *
   4:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * Copyright (C) 1994-1996, Thomas G. Lane.
   5:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * Modified 2003-2009 by Guido Vollbeding.
   6:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * This file is part of the Independent JPEG Group's software.
   7:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * For conditions of distribution and use, see the accompanying README file.
   8:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  *
   9:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * This file contains a floating-point implementation of the
  10:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * forward DCT (Discrete Cosine Transform).
  11:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  *
  12:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * This implementation should be more accurate than either of the integer
  13:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * DCT implementations.  However, it may not give the same results on all
  14:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * machines because of differences in roundoff behavior.  Speed will depend
  15:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * on the hardware's floating point capacity.
  16:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  *
  17:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * A 2-D DCT can be done by 1-D DCT on each row followed by 1-D DCT
  18:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * on each column.  Direct algorithms are also available, but they are
  19:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * much more complex and seem not to be any faster when reduced to code.
  20:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  *
  21:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * This implementation is based on Arai, Agui, and Nakajima's algorithm for
  22:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * scaled DCT.  Their original paper (Trans. IEICE E-71(11):1095) is in
  23:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * Japanese, but the algorithm is described in the Pennebaker & Mitchell
  24:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * JPEG textbook (see REFERENCES section in file README).  The following code
  25:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * is based directly on figure 4-8 in P&M.
  26:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * While an 8-point DCT cannot be done in less than 11 multiplies, it is
  27:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * possible to arrange the computation so that many of the multiplies are
  28:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * simple scalings of the final outputs.  These multiplies can then be
  29:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * folded into the multiplications or divisions by the JPEG quantization
ARM GAS  /tmp/ccKg36Sg.s 			page 2


  30:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * table entries.  The AA&N method leaves only 5 multiplies and 29 adds
  31:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * to be done in the DCT itself.
  32:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * The primary disadvantage of this method is that with a fixed-point
  33:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * implementation, accuracy is lost due to imprecise representation of the
  34:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * scaled quantization values.  However, that problem does not arise if
  35:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  * we use floating point arithmetic.
  36:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c ****  */
  37:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jfdctflt.c **** 
